# Account home directory .htaccess — the single source of truth for this
# site's URL routing.
#
# Why the home directory and not the document root: on these cPanel accounts
# the docroot (~/bid.j172.tw on the legacy host, ~/public_html on the new
# one) sits one level below the account home, and Apache merges the .htaccess
# files of every directory on the path down to the requested one, so this
# file is read for every request to either site. The docroot file
# (.remote-htaccess) carries PHP directives only and has never contained a
# single rewrite rule — the rules below are the whole of the routing, and
# until now they lived nowhere but a file somebody created by hand on the
# server on 2026-07-25.
#
# That is exactly what bit the #182 migration: the new host had no home-level
# .htaccess, so `/` answered 200 (index.php is a DirectoryIndex) while every
# other path 404'd — a symptom misleading enough to send the investigation
# after Node, pm2 and the proxy first. Tracking the file here means the next
# host rebuild gets the routing from the deploy instead of from memory.
#
# Deliberately NOT included: the cPanel-generated error_log / log_errors
# block. It names /home/<account>/logs/php.error.log outright, .htaccess
# cannot parameterise a path, and one repo now deploys two accounts — so any
# literal here is wrong on one of the two hosts by construction. The same
# reasoning already removed that block from .remote-htaccess (commit
# 714ad47). Nothing is lost by leaving it out: cPanel generates a php.ini and
# a .user.ini in each account's own document root, and both already set that
# account's correct error_log path (verified on the new host:
# /home/xiangshu/logs/php.error.log, log_errors On), so PHP goes on logging to
# the right file on both sites.
#
# Edit with care: a syntax error here takes the ENTIRE site to HTTP 500.
# After deploying, verify /, /listings, /news, /contact, /zh-CN, /en and
# /api/health all answer 200, that /news/ still 301s to /news, and that a
# real image under /uploads/ still loads (the -f condition below is what
# keeps real files ahead of the PHP front controller).

DirectoryIndex index.html index.php
RewriteEngine On
RewriteRule ^favicon\.ico$ - [L]
RewriteRule ^news/$ /news [R=301,L]
RewriteRule ^index\.php$ - [L]
# Serve real static files and directories before the legacy PHP front controller.
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} -f [OR]
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} -d
RewriteRule ^ - [L]

RewriteRule ^.*$ index.php [L,QSA]
